WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.930 --> 00:00:05.630
The Stephen King multiverse is vast so vast
that some diehard horror fans still haven't

00:00:05.630 --> 00:00:08.000
fully explored it but we're going to do our
best.

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:12.880
Here's a brief explanation of the King multiverse,
from its origins to its various realities.

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:18.060
Stephen King's work now spans over 60 novels
and dozens of short stories, many of which

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:20.580
have been adapted to the screen in one form
or another.

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:24.820
His work has influenced pop culture in ways
that most authors only dream about.

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:27.780
"I'm your number one fan."

00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:33.740
Throughout his work, King has created a full-fledged
multiverse.

00:00:34.060 --> 00:00:38.140
This multiverse is touched upon mainly in
his Dark Tower novels — a saga that offers

00:00:38.140 --> 00:00:42.390
a potentially limitless number of alternate
dimensions, pocket realities, and portals

00:00:42.390 --> 00:00:43.500
between worlds.

00:00:43.660 --> 00:00:47.780
Unfortunately, one of those worlds gave us
The Dark Tower film adaptation, but that's

00:00:47.780 --> 00:00:48.940
a story for another time.

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:54.280
Sadly, there isn't a Stephen King novel that
clearly lays out every single aspect of this

00:00:54.280 --> 00:00:57.560
multiverse... but a careful reader can slowly
start putting the pieces together.

00:00:57.880 --> 00:01:00.640
The multiverse began with a darkness known
as the Prim.

00:01:00.940 --> 00:01:06.040
From that Prim came a creator a being called
Gan whose name we first hear in The Dark Tower

00:01:06.040 --> 00:01:06.540
series.

00:01:06.760 --> 00:01:08.120
Hard to say what Gan looks like.

00:01:08.380 --> 00:01:11.840
For our purposes, let's say Gan looks like
George Burns in Oh, God!

00:01:12.620 --> 00:01:14.180
"I could even be a woman."

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:19.320
Gan is the creator of All-World, the reality
in which much of The Dark Tower saga takes

00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:19.820
place.

00:01:19.960 --> 00:01:24.580
But he's also the creator of all worlds, having
built the seemingly infinite planes of existence

00:01:24.590 --> 00:01:26.310
that form King's multiverse.

00:01:26.310 --> 00:01:30.850
He also created the Dark Tower, a massive
structure that exists physically in All-World

00:01:30.850 --> 00:01:35.040
and symbolically in all dimensions, serving
as a linchpin for all of reality.

00:01:35.180 --> 00:01:36.580
It's kind-of a big deal.

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:41.840
The plot thickens: In the final novel of the
Dark Tower series, the hero of the tale Roland

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:46.380
Deschain suspects the Dark Tower is actually
a physical manifestation of Gan.

00:01:46.600 --> 00:01:48.280
That's certainly some food for thought.

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:53.360
We know that Stephen King is fond of setting
his stories in fictional towns… but are

00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:57.660
places like Jerusalem's Lot and Castle Rock
connected to some larger fictional universe?

00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:01.740
After all, some of his novels seem to be related
to one another in mysterious ways.

00:02:01.980 --> 00:02:05.860
Remember when a character in The Dead Zone
mentions Carrie, Stephen King's first novel?

00:02:06.140 --> 00:02:10.520
Well, in The Dark Tower series, King really
embraces the idea of multiple universes in

00:02:10.520 --> 00:02:11.020
his work.

00:02:11.529 --> 00:02:15.280
In fact, the more he worked on the saga, the
more he came to realize that much of his work

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:16.980
is connected in one way or another.

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:20.680
As he wrote in the foreword to the fourth
Dark Tower book, Wizard and Glass:

00:02:21.020 --> 00:02:25.600
"I am coming to understand that Roland's world
(or worlds) actually contains all the others

00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:26.360
of my making."

00:02:26.660 --> 00:02:31.310
While writing the later volumes in his Dark
Tower series, King laid out a kind of cosmology

00:02:31.310 --> 00:02:32.840
for these various worlds.

00:02:33.040 --> 00:02:37.200
A great many of them connect or intersect,
with the Dark Tower novels serving as a kind

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:38.140
of nexus.

00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:42.740
As Stephen King's body of work expanded, so
did his fictional multiverse.

00:02:42.860 --> 00:02:48.080
In the realm of The Dark Tower, this multiverse
is vast enough to include potentially infinite

00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:52.460
worlds, and that level of vastness means there
must be spaces between those worlds, too.

00:02:53.740 --> 00:02:55.100
"Mid-world.

00:02:56.720 --> 00:02:57.820
One-way destination."

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:02.100
King's novel It explores this idea further.

00:03:02.300 --> 00:03:06.719
In fact, the book retroactively becomes part
of the Dark Tower multiverse and that's all

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:09.420
thanks to an ancient turtle spirit known as
Maturin.

00:03:09.700 --> 00:03:11.200
Who knows what Maturin looks like?

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:15.930
For our purposes, let's say Maturin is a dead
ringer for Morla in The Neverending Story.

00:03:16.160 --> 00:03:16.660
Anyway.

00:03:16.660 --> 00:03:21.040
Maturin and the creature known as It both
originate from a place called the Macroverse,

00:03:21.040 --> 00:03:23.300
which exists on a plane of reality outside
of Earth.

00:03:23.800 --> 00:03:28.279
The Macroverse is home to a variety of powerful
entities, and it even helped spawn the universe

00:03:28.280 --> 00:03:30.640
that contains King's more mainstream stories.

00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:35.780
In the first Dark Tower novel The Gunslinger,
Roland meets a young boy named Jake Chambers,

00:03:35.780 --> 00:03:39.440
who evidently arrived at All-World after dying
on his own version of Earth.

00:03:40.049 --> 00:03:44.019
In The Drawing of the Three, the second Dark
Tower novel, Roland travels through a trio

00:03:44.019 --> 00:03:48.099
of magical doors that allows him access to
that version of Earth, which has come to be

00:03:48.100 --> 00:03:51.519
known as "Keystone Earth" in the larger Dark
Tower mythos.

00:03:53.140 --> 00:03:54.840
"They're just like the quakes back home."

00:03:55.940 --> 00:04:00.400
"Yeah, what happens in one world echoes in
others."

00:04:01.060 --> 00:04:05.460
In the overarching story of The Dark Tower,
Keystone Earth is what we call the "real world."

00:04:05.940 --> 00:04:09.960
Roland visits the real world at various times
and through the eyes of different people,

00:04:09.960 --> 00:04:12.740
which helps him gain knowledge of our world
and how it works.

00:04:13.560 --> 00:04:17.699
Many of Stephen King's stories seem to take
place on Earth — rather than, say, the Keystone

00:04:17.700 --> 00:04:19.180
Earth of the Dark Tower novels.

00:04:19.520 --> 00:04:23.680
We're thinking of novels like The Shining,
It, The Dead Zone, Carrie, and a bunch of

00:04:23.680 --> 00:04:24.180
others.

00:04:24.260 --> 00:04:28.580
Well, in The Dark Tower mythology, we learn
that Earth was apparently spawned by Maturin

00:04:28.580 --> 00:04:32.740
the ancient turtle, who vomited up our planet
following a terrible stomach ache.

00:04:32.840 --> 00:04:37.300
Interestingly, several not-so subtle references
in the Dark Tower books link the story to

00:04:37.310 --> 00:04:42.120
a few of King's standalone novels... including
allusions to the monstrous Cujo and Misery's

00:04:42.120 --> 00:04:42.980
Paul Sheldon.

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:47.400
Meanwhile, Danny Torrance quotes The Dark
Tower in the novel Doctor Sleep, the sequel

00:04:47.400 --> 00:04:48.220
to The Shining.

00:04:48.360 --> 00:04:52.220
And we should mention the idea of "shining"
is brought up in The Dark Tower film, too.

00:04:55.280 --> 00:04:57.620
"His Shine is beyond anything I've ever seen."

00:04:58.080 --> 00:05:02.920
There was a six-year gap between the publication
of the fourth Dark Tower novel, 1997's Wizard

00:05:02.920 --> 00:05:07.520
and Glass, and the fifth novel in the series,
2003's Wolves of the Calla.

00:05:07.720 --> 00:05:09.680
And there's a very good reason for that.

00:05:09.820 --> 00:05:14.140
In 1999, Stephen King was struck by a van
while taking a walk near his home.

00:05:14.460 --> 00:05:15.960
The accident almost killed him.

00:05:16.400 --> 00:05:18.880
As he told The Bangor Daily News in 2014:

00:05:19.740 --> 00:05:25.900
"I was in a lot of pain, and when you're in
that situation, you don't feel much like writing."

00:05:26.480 --> 00:05:30.620
Following this near-death experience, King
decided to add a new layer of metafiction

00:05:30.639 --> 00:05:35.120
to the Dark Tower mythos: He wrote himself
into the story as a fictional character — a

00:05:35.120 --> 00:05:36.300
mind-blowing move.

00:05:40.700 --> 00:05:44.960
In the sixth Dark Tower novel, Song of Susannah,
Roland actually meets Stephen King.

00:05:45.260 --> 00:05:49.140
We soon learn that King is feeding Roland
the knowledge he needs to complete his quest.

00:05:49.140 --> 00:05:53.120
And if this version of Stephen King dies,
Roland's quest will end in failure.

00:05:54.320 --> 00:05:58.400
Out of all the film and TV adaptations of
Stephen King's work, only The Dark Tower and

00:05:58.400 --> 00:06:01.440
Castle Rock have really embraced the idea
of a multiverse.

00:06:01.810 --> 00:06:05.960
In The Dark Tower film, plenty of characters
take jaunts between All-World and Keystone

00:06:05.960 --> 00:06:06.460
Earth.

00:06:06.620 --> 00:06:11.100
And in the first season of Castle Rock, the
show introduces the multiverse as a key concept.

00:06:11.540 --> 00:06:15.639
It seems as though the TV version of Castle
Rock exists in some alternate reality that's

00:06:15.640 --> 00:06:18.720
distinct from the Castle Rock featured in
novels like The Dead Zone.

00:06:25.760 --> 00:06:30.199
One thing we know for sure: As long as Stephen
King keeps writing, his multiverse will continue

00:06:30.200 --> 00:06:34.160
to expand, offering us plenty of alternate
realities to explore.

